    stories: [
      {
        kind: 'parable', tone: 'solemn',
        title: 'The Eight Hundred Nobody Mentions',
        body: 'Uncle Ray lent his nephew eight hundred dollars in the spring for a transmission. There was no paper, because it was family, and there was no date, because putting a date on it would have felt like an insult. The nephew meant to pay it in July. In July there was a deposit on an apartment. He meant to pay it in September. In September there was a laptop for school. By November something had changed that neither of them could have pointed to. The money stopped being a debt in the nephew’s mind and became a gift, retroactively, without anybody deciding that. In Uncle Ray’s mind it stayed exactly what it was. The first Thanksgiving was fine. The second Thanksgiving, Ray was a little short with him about something unrelated, and the nephew thought his uncle had gotten harder to be around. By the fourth year they had drifted far enough apart that the eight hundred dollars was no longer big enough to explain it, and so nobody looked there. Ray would have taken twenty dollars a month. He said so once, to his wife, in the kitchen, and never to the nephew. The nephew would have paid twenty dollars a month for forty months without noticing it. He never knew that was available. Neither of them was a wicked man. One of them simply stopped intending, quietly, without ever saying it out loud, and the not-saying is what did the damage — not the eight hundred, which by then was less than what he spent in a good month on things he would not remember.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'testimony', tone: 'hopeful',
        title: 'The Call I Did Not Want to Make',
        body: 'I owed a man in my congregation three thousand dollars and I could not pay it that year. Not would not. Could not. The work had gone sideways and I was choosing between his money and the light bill, and I chose the light bill every month for seven months. What I did in those seven months was not decide to cheat him. What I did was not call him. And because I did not call him, I started avoiding the row he sat in, and then I started leaving before the fellowship hall, and by the fifth month I was thinking about which service to attend based on a man I loved and owed. My wife finally said the sentence that ended it. She said: he does not know any of this. She was right. In his mind there was a loan, outstanding, to a brother he trusted. All the rest of it was happening entirely inside me. So I called him and I said the whole thing plainly, including the part where I had been avoiding him, which was worse to say than the money. He said he had wondered. Then he said something I have repeated a hundred times since. He said: I never needed it back by a date. I just needed to know you still meant to. I paid him over the next fourteen months. The debt was the easy part. The seven months of silence cost more than the three thousand did, and the silence was the only part that was ever mine to fix.',
      },
    ],
    lesson: 'Psalms 37:21 is the verse most often used to prove that debt is sin, and it is worth reading slowly, because it proves something else and the something else is harder. "The wicked borroweth, and payeth not again: but the righteous sheweth mercy, and giveth" (Psalms 37:21). The condemning predicate is PAYETH NOT AGAIN. Borrowing occurs in the clause; it is not what is judged. What is judged is the obligation that stops being honoured. That distinction is not a technicality, and it has real pastoral consequences. A believer taught that owing is itself sin will hide his position, avoid counsel and decide badly in private, because bringing it into the light would mean confessing a sin he has not actually committed. A believer taught that UNPAID OBLIGATION is the sin will bring the same numbers into the light, where they can be worked. Then look at the other half of the verse, which is skipped more often than any part of it. The righteous man set opposite the wicked borrower is not described as debt-free. He is described as merciful, and giving. David is not contrasting debt with no debt. He is contrasting a closed hand with an open one, which is a different axis altogether, and it is the axis this whole department has been walking since the first course. Four verses later David adds the testimony of a long life: "I have been young, and now am old; yet have I not seen the righteous forsaken, nor his seed begging bread. He is ever merciful, and lendeth; and his seed is blessed" (Psalms 37:25-26). The righteous end of this psalm is a LENDER. That is the same place Deuteronomy 28 pointed to by another road — not a verdict on the borrower but a direction he can walk. Now make it operational, and be honest about where the failures actually occur. It is almost never the mortgage. A mortgage is documented, scheduled, amortised and very difficult to forget; the system itself keeps you honest. The obligations that fail this verse are the undocumented ones. Money from a relative that both sides stopped mentioning, so that it quietly became a gift without anyone ever saying the word. A loan from a brother in the congregation that neither party will now raise, because raising it has become awkward. A service promised in exchange for something already received. A bill postponed so many times that postponement hardened into policy. None of those will appear on a credit report, and every one of them is exactly what the psalm names. So the instrument is a complete list — formal and informal in the same column, with what FULL repayment looks like beside each, and a date. Then one more pass, and this is the one that costs something: mark the obligations you have stopped INTENDING to pay. Not the ones you cannot pay yet; the ones you have quietly written off in your own mind while the other party has not. That mark is the subject of Psalms 37:21. The balance is not. And the mark can be removed this week, because intention is the single part of any financing that a man governs entirely by himself, whatever his income, whatever his rate, and whatever else is still outstanding.',
